NVMe Basics
NVMe = Non-Volatile Memory Express
It's a protocol designed for fast SSDs. NVMe can run over:

Direct PCIe (local SSD—very fast)
NVMe/FC (Fibre Channel fabric—enterprise SANs)
NVMe/TCP (Ethernet—modern, flexible)
NVMe/TCP vs NVMe/FC Comparison
Feature	NVMe/TCP	NVMe/FC
Network	Standard Ethernet	Dedicated Fibre Channel
Latency	Higher (100-400μs+)	Lower (sub-100μs)
Bandwidth	10/25/40/100Gbps+	16/32/64Gbps per channel
CPU Usage	Higher (TCP/IP stack overhead)	Lower (HBA offload)
I/O Consistency	Variable (shared network)	Very consistent (isolated)
Hardware	Standard NIC + switch	FC HBA + FC switch
Cost	Lower	Higher
Deployment	Easy, scalable	Complex, traditional SAN
Best For	Cloud, hyper-converged, cost-sensitive	Mission-critical, ultra-low latency DBs
Bottom Line: NVMe/FC = performance champion but expensive. NVMe/TCP = good enough for most, cheaper and more flexible.

NVMe/FC Configuration & Troubleshooting
Configuration Steps (NetApp ONTAP)
Enable NVMe Service

bash
vserver nvme enable -vserver <SVM_name>
Create NVMe LIFs (Logical Interfaces)

bash
network interface create -vserver <SVM> -lif <LIF_name> \
  -role data -data-protocol nvme-fc \
  -home-node <node> -home-port <port>
Create NVMe Subsystem

bash
vserver nvme subsystem create -vserver <SVM> -subsystem <subsystem_name>
Map Host to Subsystem (using host NQN)

bash
# First, get the host NQN from the server
# Then add it to the subsystem:
vserver nvme subsystem host add -vserver <SVM> \
  -subsystem <subsystem_name> -host-nqn <host_nqn>
Map Namespaces (LUNs)

bash
vserver nvme namespace create -vserver <SVM> \
  -namespace-path /vol/<vol_name>/<namespace> \
  -size <size> -ostype linux

vserver nvme namespace map -vserver <SVM> \
  -namespace-path /vol/<vol_name>/<namespace> \
  -subsystem <subsystem_name>
Verify Setup

bash
vserver nvme show
vserver nvme subsystem show
vserver nvme namespace show
network interface show -data-protocol nvme-fc
Host-Side NVMe/FC Commands (Linux)
bash
# Discover NVMe/FC targets
nvme discover-controllers -t fc

# List discovered subsystems
nvme subsystem-list

# Connect to target
nvme connect -t fc -n <subsystem_nqn> \
  --traddr=<target_wwpn> --trsvcid=4420

# List connected namespaces
nvme list

# Show path info
nvme list-subsys
Common NVMe/FC Issues & Fixes
Issue	Cause	Troubleshooting
Host can't discover targets	WWPN zoning missing or host NQN not registered	Check FC switch zones; Verify vserver nvme subsystem host show
Connection drops	Path loss, fabric errors, firmware mismatch	Check switch port stats for CRC/buffer errors; Verify all components at compatible versions
Slow I/O	QoS policy limit, fabric congestion, multipath issue	Check qos policy show; Monitor switch fabric errors; Verify multipath config
Host sees offline namespaces	Namespace not mapped to subsystem	Run vserver nvme namespace map to add namespace
Port status down	Cabling, switch port disabled, FC license missing	Check cabling; Verify switch port is up; Confirm FC is licensed
