NVMe/TCP Configuration & Troubleshooting
Configuration Steps (NetApp ONTAP)
Enable NVMe Service

bash
vserver nvme enable -vserver <SVM_name>
Create NVMe/TCP LIF

bash
network interface create -vserver <SVM> -lif <LIF_name> \
  -role data -data-protocol nvme-tcp \
  -home-node <node> -home-port <port> \
  -address <IP> -netmask <netmask>
Create Subsystem & Map (same as NVMe/FC)

bash
vserver nvme subsystem create -vserver <SVM> -subsystem <name>
vserver nvme subsystem host add -vserver <SVM> \
  -subsystem <name> -host-nqn <host_nqn>
Create & Map Namespaces (same as NVMe/FC)

bash
vserver nvme namespace create -vserver <SVM> \
  -namespace-path /vol/<vol_name>/<ns_name> -size <size>
vserver nvme namespace map -vserver <SVM> \
  -namespace-path /vol/<vol_name>/<ns_name> \
  -subsystem <subsystem_name>
Verify Setup

bash
network interface show -data-protocol nvme-tcp
vserver nvme show
Host-Side NVMe/TCP Commands (Linux)
bash
# Discover NVMe/TCP targets (default port 4420)
nvme discover -t tcp -a <ONTAP_LIF_IP> -s 4420

# Connect to target
nvme connect -t tcp -a <ONTAP_LIF_IP> -s 4420 \
  -n <subsystem_nqn>

# List connected devices
nvme list

# Show namespace details
nvme subsystem-list
nvme list-subsys
Common NVMe/TCP Issues & Fixes
Issue	Cause	Troubleshooting
Can't discover subsystems	Network unreachable, LIF down, wrong IP/port	Ping LIF IP; Verify LIF is online; Use nc -zv <IP> 4420 to test port
Connection timeout	Firewall blocking port 4420, MTU issues, network latency	Check firewall rules; Test with telnet <IP> 4420; Verify MTU 9000
High latency/slow I/O	Network congestion, CPU bottleneck on host, MTU fragmentation	Enable jumbo frames (MTU 9000); Check host CPU/network usage; Use iperf3 to test bandwidth
Namespace not visible	Namespace not created or mapped	Run vserver nvme namespace show to verify existence and mapping
Intermittent disconnects	Network instability, VLAN misconfiguration, driver issues	Check VLAN tagging; Verify host driver is latest; Monitor for packet loss

NetApp NVMe/TCP Troubleshooting Commands
bash
# Check NVMe/TCP LIFs
