Networking Tuning for NVMe/TCP
bash
# Enable jumbo frames (on both host and NetApp)
# Linux host:
ip link set dev <NIC> mtu 9000

# ONTAP:
network port modify -node <node> -port <port> -mtu 9000

# Verify MTU
ip link show
network port show -fields mtu
StorageGRID & NVMe
StorageGrid is object storage, not block storage, so it doesn't use NVMe/FC or NVMe/TCP directly for client data. However:

StorageGrid Appliances (like SG6060, SGF6024, SG6160) internally use NVMe SSDs to accelerate metadata access and cache.
Newer appliances like SGF6112 are all-NVMe for high-performance workloads.
StorageGrid itself connects to clients via S3/Swift APIs over HTTP/HTTPS (not NVMe).
Key Point: If you're supporting StorageGrid customers, you won't configure NVMe/FC or NVMe/TCP directly—the appliances handle that internally. But you'll see it when supporting E-Series arrays (block storage) or ONTAP systems (block + file).

E-Series & NVMe/TCP/FC
NetApp E-Series systems (EF600, E5700, etc.) are block storage arrays that do support NVMe/FC and NVMe/TCP. Configuration is similar to ONTAP but via SANtricity System Manager instead:

Enable NVMe/FC or NVMe/TCP in Settings → System → Feature Settings → NVMe
Configure host ports and assign IP addresses (for TCP)
Create host groups using host NQN
Map volumes to host groups
Verify via nvme list on the host
Key Takeaways for Support
NVMe/FC = High performance, mission-critical, Fibre Channel-based
NVMe/TCP = Cost-effective,flexible, Ethernet-based

Always check:
Host NQN matches subsystem registration
Namespaces are created and mapped
Networking/zoning is correct
Firmware/driver versions are compatible

IStorageGrid doesn't use NVMe/FC/TCP for client access (S3 only)
E-Series and ONTAP systems use both protocols extensively


Overview
NVMe is the protocol for SSD-backed storage. It can run over:

NVMe/FC (Fibre Channel)
NVMe/TCP (Ethernet)
Local PCIe (direct attached)
Common use cases:

ONTAP block storage
E-Series block storage
Host connectivity to array controllers
High-performance SSD workloads

Quick Comparison
Protocol	Transport	Common Hardware	Performance	Typical Use
NVMe/FC	Fibre Channel	FC HBA + FC switch	Very low latency, very high performance	Enterprise SAN
NVMe/TCP	Ethernet	NIC + switch	Lower latency than general TCP workloads, flexible	Cost-effective, IP-based
NVMe local	PCIe	Internal SSD	Lowest latency	Local NVMe drives
Key Terms
NQN: NVMe Qualified Name
Namespace: Logical storage unit exposed to the host
Subsystem: NVMe target or controller group
WWPN: Fibre Channel worldwide port name
Transport: FC or TCP
LIF: Logical interface (ONTAP)
Host: Server connecting to storage
NVMe/FC Reference
Host-side commands (Linux)
bash
# Show host NQN
cat /etc/nvme/hostnqn

# Discover NVMe/FC target
nvme discover -t fc -a <target_ip_or_fabric_info>

# Connect to subsystem
nvme connect -t fc -n <subsystem_nqn> --traddr=<target_wwpn>

# List connected NVMe devices
